---
/**
 * /nuclear-bomb-map/{city}/ — one city, one page, three kinds of number.
 *
 * WHAT COMES FROM WHERE (this is the whole contract of the page):
 *
 *   1. The five distances, at 15 kt / 100 kt / 1 Mt   -> src/data/effects.json,
 *      looked up by get(). Not recomputed here. Same values as /blast-radius/,
 *      byte for byte, because both read the same JSON.
 *   2. People inside a 10 km disc                       -> WorldPop, fetched once
 *      by scripts/build_city_pop.py and cached in src/data/city-pop.json. This
 *      is the only number on the page that is not in the manual, and it is the
 *      only one that depends on where the city is.
 *   3. People inside the 5 psi ring at 100 kt            -> same WorldPop request,
 *      a different radius. Computed, never derived by subtracting one ring from
 *      another, and never subtracted from (2) to imply a survivor count.
 *
 * Air burst only. The thermal rows have no contact-burst counterpart (Fig. 7.42
 * is drawn for air bursts, §7.41 p.290), so a page that mixed the two would be
 * inventing a number rather than reporting one.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import {
  YIELDS,
  EFFECT_ROWS,
  get,
  citationLine,
  fmtKm,
  manual,
} from '../../lib/effects';
import { cities, PUBLISHED_CITY_SLUGS, coordText, type City } from '../../lib/cities';
import {
  cityPopulation,
  peopleWithin,
  fmtPeople,
  populationSentence,
  popSource,
  CITY_RADIUS_KM,
} from '../../lib/city-pop';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return cities
    .filter((c) => PUBLISHED_CITY_SLUGS.includes(c.slug))
    .map((c) => ({ params: { city: c.slug }, props: { city: c } }));
}

interface Props {
  city: City;
}

const { city } = Astro.props;
const path = `/nuclear-bomb-map/${city.slug}/`;

const cityPop = cityPopulation(city);
const psi5Pop = peopleWithin(city, get('overpressure_5psi', 'air', 100)!.radius_km);
const psi1Pop = peopleWithin(city, get('overpressure_1psi', 'air', 100)!.radius_km);
const fireballPop = peopleWithin(city, get('fireball', 'air', 100)!.radius_km);
const share = (100 * psi5Pop) / cityPop;  // percent, one decimal
const [latText, lonText] = coordText(city).split(', ');

// 标题模板：`Nuclear Bomb Effects on {City}: 15 kt to 1 Mt`
// 不写「15 kt, 100 kt, 1 Mt」——那串词与 /blast-radius/ 的 title 有 6 词重合，
// 两个页面会在同一批查询里互相压。城市名承担唯一性。
const title =
  `Nuclear Bomb Effects on ${city.name}: 15 kt to 1 Mt`;
const description =
  `What 15 kt, 100 kt and 1 Mt air bursts reach over ${city.name}: 5 psi to ` +
  `${fmtKm(get('overpressure_5psi', 'air', 100)!.radius_km)} km, 1 psi to ` +
  `${fmtKm(get('overpressure_1psi', 'air', 100)!.radius_km)} km, and ` +
  `${fmtPeople(psi5Pop)} people inside the 5 psi ring.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: `Nuclear weapon effects over ${city.name}`,
  description: description,
  url: `https://apocalab.com${path}`,
  spatialCoverage: { '@type': 'Place', name: city.name },
  license: 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/',
  isBasedOn: {
    '@type': 'Book',
    name: 'The Effects of Nuclear Weapons',
    edition: '3rd',
    datePublished: '1977',
    identifier: 'doi:10.2172/6852629',
  },
};
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path={path} current="map" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <p class="eyebrow">
    Air burst · 15 kt, 100 kt, 1 Mt · {manual.title}, {manual.year}
  </p>
  <h1>Nuclear Bomb Effects on {city.name} — 15 kt, 100 kt and 1 Mt</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Five distances over {city.name}, plus two population counts. The distances are the same
    figures as the <a href="/blast-radius/">nuclear bomb blast radius</a> table: read off
    Fig.&nbsp;3.73c and Fig.&nbsp;7.42, scaled only by the cube-root law the manual prints on
    p.&nbsp;101. The population counts are the one thing the manual cannot supply — 1977 has no
    gridded population surface — so they come from WorldPop and are labelled as such.
  </p>

  <h2 id="distances">What each yield reaches over {city.name}</h2>
  <p>
    Measured from {coordText(city)}, the GeoNames point for {city.name} — the same point the
    population radius is drawn around, so the two halves of this page describe one place.
    Every row is an air burst at the height of burst that puts the contour at its furthest, which
    is how the manual reads its own charts (p.&nbsp;114); those heights are printed in the
    <a href="/blast-radius/">nuclear bomb blast radius</a> table, column by column.
  </p>

  <p class="tbl-hint">Scroll the table sideways →</p>
  <div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table class="data">
      <caption>Distance from ground zero, in kilometres. Air burst.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Effect</th>
          {YIELDS.map((y) => <th scope="col">{y.toLocaleString('en-US')} kt</th>)}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          EFFECT_ROWS.map((row) => {
            const first = get(row.id, 'air', 15)!;
            return (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  {row.name}
                  <span class="th-sub">{citationLine(first)}</span>
                </th>
                {YIELDS.map((y) => (
                  <td>
                    <span class="v">{fmtKm(get(row.id, 'air', y)!.radius_km)}</span>
                    <span class="th-sub">{fmtPeople(peopleWithin(city, get(row.id, 'air', y)!.radius_km))} inside</span>
                  </td>
                ))}
              </tr>
            );
          })
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note">
    <strong>The small number under each radius is people, not area.</strong>
    It is the WorldPop count inside that ring, from the same request as the 10 km figure below.
    At 1 Mt the two outer rings extend past the 10 km disc, so they are larger than the city
    figure by construction — the rings and the city are two different questions, not two
    measurements of one thing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="five-psi">How many people are inside the 5 psi ring</h2>
  <p>
    At 100 kt the 5 psi contour reaches {fmtKm(get('overpressure_5psi', 'air', 100)!.radius_km)} km
    over {city.name}. Inside that circle live {fmtPeople(psi5Pop)} people — {share.toFixed(1)}% of
    everyone inside the 10 km disc, concentrated into about a tenth of its area, because cities are
    not uniformly populated. The 1 psi ring reaches {fmtKm(get('overpressure_1psi', 'air', 100)!.radius_km)}
    km and holds {fmtPeople(psi1Pop)}; the fireball at breakaway, {fmtKm(get('fireball', 'air', 100)!.radius_km)}
    km, holds {fmtPeople(fireballPop)}.
  </p>

  <h2 id="population">What the 10 km population is counting</h2>
  <p id="population-convention">{populationSentence(city, latText!, lonText!)}</p>
  <p>
    Two things follow from that sentence. The count is a disc, not a city: it takes in whatever
    happens to be within {CITY_RADIUS_KM} km of the point, including water, parks and the next
    municipality over. And it is a 2020 estimate, not a census — {popSource.name} is a modelled
    surface, so the figure is reproducible by anyone who re-runs the same query, which is the only
    property that matters here.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">What this page does not say</h2>
  <p>
    It gives distances and head counts. It does not estimate deaths, injuries, or damage to any
    particular building, because the manual supplies no casualty model and this site does not
    invent one. It ignores terrain, shelter, time of day and weather, all of which move the real
    answer by more than the last digit printed here. It covers one warhead, not a salvo. And at
    1 Mt the two outer rings sit above the 5,000 ft ceiling Fig.&nbsp;3.73c was computed for
    (§3.73, p.&nbsp;110), so they are the edge of what that chart supports.
  </p>
  <p>
    For all three yields side by side, with the burst height and the printed page behind every
    cell, the <a href="/blast-radius/">nuclear bomb blast radius</a> table is one page. To move the
    same rings around on real ground, the <a href="/nuclear-bomb-map/">nuclear bomb map</a> does
    that for {cities.length} cities. To check any of it against the book, the audit trail is under
    <a href="/methodology/">where these numbers come from</a>; the interactive version of this
    single city is the <a href="/">nuke simulator</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
